{
  "title": "diaspora* \u2014 social (13,654\u2605)",
  "content": "Project: diaspora*\nDomain: social | Scale: large | Stars: 13,654\nProblem: A privacy-aware, distributed, open source social network that gives users ownership and control of their data through federated architecture, avoiding centralized corporate silos.\nTarget users: Privacy-conscious users, Pod administrators, Open source developers, Communities seeking decentralized social platforms\nCore features: Federated social networking; User data ownership and privacy; Posts, shares, and messaging; Markdown composition with mentions and hashtags; Image uploads and gallery views; Pod installation and administration; Inter-pod federation protocol\nIntegrations: ActivityPub/Diaspora protocol for federation, Markdown rendering with syntax extensions, Image processing and cropping\nTech stack: JavaScript with Backbone.js, jQuery, Bootstrap Markdown + Ruby on Rails + PostgreSQL (inferred from Rails convention) + Self-hosted pod architecture\nKey libraries: Backbone.js, jQuery, markdown-it, markdown-it-diaspora-mention, markdown-it-hashtag, fine-uploader, cropperjs, blueimp-gallery, highlight.js\nArchitecture: federated\nDesign lessons:\n- Federated architecture enables user data sovereignty by allowing independent pod instances to communicate via protocol, avoiding single point of failure and corporate control.\n- Markdown-first composition with rich plugin ecosystem (mentions, hashtags, embeds, footnotes) creates expressive user content while maintaining security through sanitization.\n- Client-side rendering with Backbone.js and jQuery reflects early 2010s patterns; significant opportunity for modernization to contemporary frontend frameworks.\n- Community-driven development prioritizes transparency and accessibility, with extensive wiki documentation and multiple contribution pathways beyond code.",
